  "worked_solution": "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,\u00ac(A\u2227\u00acB)\\,$ | $\\,\u00acA\u2227B\\,$ | $\\,\u00acA\u2228B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $\u00ac(A\u2227\u00acB)$ and $\u00acA\u2228B$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
